    <h1>About This Journal</h1>

    <p>
      <em>Bedroom Dialectics</em> is a quiet space to untangle ideas that feel too long for quick social feeds and too unresolved for formal papers. Most pieces come out of ordinary observations: sitting at a desk late at night, noticing friction in daily habits, or tracing the physical systems and historical events that shape how we live.
    </p>

    <h2>What I Write About</h2>

    <p>
      Essays are published in both <strong>English</strong> and <strong>Bahasa Indonesia</strong>, usually revolving around three themes:
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Craft and cognition:</strong> Dealing with taste that runs ahead of mechanical skill, the trap of reorganizing tools instead of working, and how difficulty with complex topics usually traces back to missed prerequisites.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>History and political economy:</strong> Grounding political shifts in physical reality, like early railway labor unions in colonial Java, the food logistics behind regime changes, or the repair ships maintaining undersea fiber-optic lines.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Daily life and public spaces:</strong> The unhurried sociology of roadside coffee stalls at 2 AM, the kindness of messy city neighborhoods over sterile master-plans, and the relief of leaving certain questions unanswered.
    </p>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>

    <p>
      This site is statically generated using <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Astro</a>, rendered in plain HTML and CSS, and set in <em>IBM Plex Mono</em>. There are no tracking scripts, popups, or feed algorithms.
    </p>

    <p>
      New essays are published via the <a href="/rss.xml">RSS Feed</a>. If a piece brings up a question, disagreement, or idea of your own, you are always welcome to reach out.
    </p>
  </div>
